      "finding": null,
      "explanation": "The skill reads, extracts, and processes PDF form data \u2014 which commonly contains highly sensitive personal information such as names, addresses, Social Security Numbers, financial data, and medical information \u2014 without any privacy warnings, data minimization guidance, or user consent prompts. The skill instructs the AI to analyze field contents visually (via page image conversion) and create intermediate JSON files (`field_info.json`, `field_values.json`) that may persist on disk with sensitive data in plaintext. There is no mention of secure handling, temporary file cleanup, or encryption of these intermediate artifacts.",
      "remediation": "1. Add explicit privacy warnings informing users that form data (potentially PII) will be read and stored in intermediate files. 2. Instruct the agent to delete intermediate JSON and image files after the fill operation completes. 3. Recommend users avoid using this skill with highly sensitive documents in untrusted environments. 4. Consider encrypting or memory-only handling of intermediate data structures containing PII. 5. Document the data flow clearly so users understand what data is written where.",
      "code_snippet": null,
      "intent": null,
      "tags": []
    },
    {
      "id": "SQP-2",
      "finding_id": "finding-0be494153f3d4668888181872d27377b",
      "category": null,
      "pattern": null,
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "confidence": 0.72,
      "location": {
        "file": "reference.md",
        "start_line": 340,
        "end_line": 340
      },
      "finding": null,
      "explanation": "The bash command example `qpdf --password=secret123 --decrypt encrypted.pdf decrypted.pdf` hardcodes a plaintext password directly in a shell command without any accompanying security warning. Developers who copy this pattern may hardcode real passwords in shell scripts, Makefiles, CI/CD configs, or documentation that could be committed to version control or exposed in shell history. This is a negligent documentation practice that could propagate insecure patterns.",
      "remediation": "Replace the hardcoded password with a placeholder and add a security warning. For example, use `--password=\"$PDF_PASSWORD\"` and note that passwords should be stored in environment variables or secret managers, never hardcoded in scripts or committed to source control. Add a comment like '# WARNING: Never hardcode passwords. Use environment variables instead.'",
      "code_snippet": null,
      "intent": null,
      "tags": []
    },
    {
      "id": "SQP-2",
      "finding_id": "finding-fec77b7aedca4312a61bef4d16c2caea",
      "category": null,
      "pattern": null,
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "location": {
        "file": "reference.md",
        "start_line": 577,
        "end_line": 577
      },
      "finding": null,
      "explanation": "The Python decryption example `reader.decrypt(\"password\")` uses a hardcoded literal string as a password placeholder without any security warning. While 'password' is obviously a placeholder, developers new to the library may use this pattern with real credentials, embedding passwords directly in source code. This can lead to credential exposure through version control, log files, or error messages if the string is echoed.",
      "remediation": "Replace the hardcoded string with a reference to a secure input mechanism and add a warning comment. For example: `password = os.environ.get('PDF_PASSWORD') or getpass.getpass('Enter PDF password: ')` followed by `reader.decrypt(password)`. Add a comment: '# WARNING: Never hardcode passwords in source code. Use environment variables or secure input prompts.'",
      "code_snippet": null,
      "intent": null,
      "tags": []
    }
